---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import AlertBox from '../../components/ui/AlertBox.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/ui/ComparisonTable.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../components/ui/FAQSection.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'ratings', label: 'Standby vs prime vs continuous' },
  { id: 'testing', label: 'How ratings are tested' },
  { id: 'derating', label: 'Altitude & temperature derating' },
  { id: 'surge-mult', label: 'Motor surge multipliers' },
  { id: 'method', label: 'Pro load-calc method' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: '7 sizing mistakes' },
  { id: 'over-under', label: 'Over vs under-sizing' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="Generator Ratings Explained: Standby vs Prime, Derating & Sizing Method"
  description="Standby vs prime vs continuous ratings (ISO 8528), altitude and temperature derating with worked math, motor surge multipliers, pro load-calc steps, and sizing mistakes."
  category="Sizing" categoryHref="/sizing/" updated="2026-10-03" readingTime="12 min read" toc={toc}
  related={[
    { href: '/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/', title: 'What Size Generator Do I Need?', description: 'Full sizing walkthrough.', category: 'Sizing' },
    { href: '/guides/power-quality-thd-surge-protection/', title: 'Power Quality, THD & Surge', description: 'Clean power for electronics.', category: 'Buying Guides' },
  ]}
  prev={{ href: '/sizing/understanding-generator-ratings/', label: 'Ratings & Sizing Method' }}
  next={{ href: '/sizing/what-size-for-2000-sq-ft/', label: 'Size for 2,000 Sq Ft' }}
>
  <section id="ratings"><h2>Standby vs prime vs continuous (ISO 8528)</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Generator duty ratings" headers={['', 'Standby (ESP)', 'Prime (PRP)', 'Continuous (COP)']} rows={[
    ['Use', 'Emergency backup only', 'Unlimited hrs, variable load', 'Unlimited hrs, constant load'],
    ['Annual cap', '~200 h (some 500 h)', 'Unlimited', 'Unlimited'],
    ['Avg load factor', '≤70%', 'Variable', 'Constant'],
    ['Overload', 'None allowed', '10% for 1 h per 12 h', 'None allowed'],
    ['Home relevance', 'Every residential standby', 'Off-grid primary, sites', 'Peak shaving, base load'],
  ]} />
  <p class="mt-3">Same-machine relationship: prime ≈ 90% of standby, continuous ≈ 70–80% — a 22 kW standby unit is ~20 kW prime, ~17 kW continuous. Residential buyers pay for standby and must <em>use</em> standby: off-grid primary duty on a standby-rated unit voids warranties and kills engines early. Typical homes log 20–50 h/year — nowhere near the 200-hour ceiling.</p></section>

  <section id="testing"><h2>How ratings are tested (and why nameplates are honest)</h2>
  <p>ISO 8528-1 defines the ratings; testing runs load banks at rated power while monitoring temperature, fuel burn at multiple load points, voltage/frequency stability under load steps, and emissions — at reference conditions of 77°F, ~328 ft altitude, 30% humidity. Your backyard is not the lab, which is exactly why derating exists.</p></section>

  <section id="derating"><h2>Altitude & temperature derating (with math)</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Derating rules" headers={['Factor', 'Rule', '22 kW example']} rows={[
    ['Altitude', '−3.5% per 1,000 ft above 500 ft', 'At 5,000 ft: 22 × (1 − 0.035 × 4.5) = 18.5 kW (−16%)'],
    ['Temperature', '−1% per 10°F above 77°F', 'At 95°F: 22 × (1 − 0.01 × 1.8) = 21.6 kW (−2%)'],
    ['Combined', 'Multiply both', '5,000 ft + 95°F: 22 × 0.84 × 0.98 ≈ 18.1 kW (−18%)'],
  ]} />
  <AlertBox type="warning" title="Mountain buyers read this">Above ~10,000 ft consult the manufacturer (tuning/turbocharging territory), and gas engines derate harder than diesel on thin air. A Denver home and a Miami home do not buy the same 22 kW — size to <em>derated</em> capacity, then confirm with the local dealer.</AlertBox></section>

  <section id="surge-mult"><h2>Motor surge multipliers (why starting type matters)</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Starting surge by motor type" headers={['Starting method', 'Surge vs running', 'Where you meet it']} rows={[
    ['Direct-on-line (DOL)', '~7×', 'Well pumps, basic compressors'],
    ['Star-delta', '~2.5×', 'Three-phase with soft start'],
    ['Electronic soft starter', '~3×', 'Retrofitted compressors'],
    ['VFD drive', '~1.5×', 'Modern HVAC, VFD well pumps'],
    ['Inverter compressor', '~1.2×', 'Inverter HVAC, mini-splits'],
  ]} />
  <p class="mt-3">This table is the quiet reason two "3-ton AC" homes size differently: a DOL compressor surges ~7× while an inverter sibling barely ripples. Ask the nameplate, not the tonnage.</p></section>

  <section id="method"><h2>Pro load-calc method in 7 steps</h2>
  <ol><li>Inventory every backed-up device (nameplates, not guesses).</li><li>Sum continuous running watts.</li><li>Identify the single largest motor; add its starting surge (multiplier above).</li><li>Apply diversity (×0.7–0.8 — not everything runs at once).</li><li>Add 10–25% headroom for growth and derating.</li><li>Select the next standard size at or above the result.</li><li>Verify derated capacity at your altitude/temperature still covers the peak.</li></ol>
  <p>Manufacturer selectors (e.g., Generac's online tool: home size → circuits → appliances → fuel → coverage) follow this same skeleton with built-in diversity — a fine starting point that a licensed calculation must still confirm for large or complex homes.</p></section>

  <section id="mistakes"><h2>7 sizing mistakes, each expensive differently</h2>
  <ul><li><strong>Running watts only:</strong> trips the instant the AC starts — add the largest surge.</li><li><strong>kW vs kVA confusion:</strong> motors billed in kVA (kW ÷ power factor ~0.8) need ~20% more headroom.</li><li><strong>Gross oversizing:</strong> +10 kW unneeded costs $3,000–$5,000 and idles below efficient loading.</li><li><strong>Wrong duty rating:</strong> standby unit as primary power — voided warranty, early death.</li><li><strong>No diversity factor:</strong> nameplate-stacking oversizes 30–50%.</li><li><strong>Ignoring derating:</strong> the 5,000-ft 22 kW is an 18.5 kW.</li><li><strong>No growth margin:</strong> the future EV charger or pool has nowhere to go.</li></ul></section>

  <section id="over-under"><h2>Over vs under-sizing, priced</h2>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Over versus under sizing" headers={['', 'Oversized', 'Undersized']} rows={[
    ['Cost signal', '+$3,000–$5,000 per spare 10 kW', 'Breaker trips, stalled motors, flicker'],
    ['Reliability', 'Low-load carbon buildup (chronic <25%)', 'Overheating, unstable V/Hz, shortened life'],
    ['Warranty', 'Safe', 'Over-capacity operation can void'],
    ['Fix', 'Load management to keep loading healthy', 'Shed loads, add management, or upsize'],
    ['Verdict', '10–25% margin, never 50–100%', 'Never — size the surge'],
  ]} />
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-600">Note: "wet stacking" (unburned-fuel buildup) is properly a diesel/light-load phenomenon — gas air-cooled units suffer chronic light loading differently (carbon, moisture, efficiency). Either way the prescription matches: correct size plus healthy loading.</p></section>

  <section id="faq"><span class="sr-only">FAQ anchor</span></section>
  <FAQSection items={[
    { q: 'What does standby-rated mean?', a: 'Maximum emergency-backup power, capped around 200 hours/year at ≤70% average load with zero overload allowance. All residential standby units are standby-rated (ISO 8528 ESP) — use them as backup, not primary power.' },
    { q: 'How much power do I lose at altitude?', a: 'Roughly 3.5% per 1,000 ft above 500 ft, plus 1% per 10°F above 77°F. A 22 kW unit at 5,000 ft and 95°F delivers about 18 kW — size up or manage loads to match derated output.' },
    { q: 'Is a bigger generator always safer?', a: 'No. Beyond a 10–25% margin, extra kilowatts buy cost, fuel burn, and low-load inefficiency — not reliability. Undersizing fails loudly; oversizing fails expensively.' },
  ]} />
  <section id="conclusion"><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Ratings tell you the duty, derating tells you the real kilowatts, surge math tells you the peak — run all three, then <a href="/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">work the full example</a> and get the licensed calculation.</p></section>
</ArticleLayout>
